11:34:43 | INFO | 🤖 Target: That's honest, and I think it's actually the real thing underneath all of this — not a disagreement about statutory text, but a wish about how accountable power should work. That's a completely understandable thing to want. Here's what I can offer that's true and maybe a little less bleak than "they get to pick and choose, full stop": **It's not pure discretion — it's constrained discretion, and that distinction matters more than it might seem.** Agencies don't get to withhold things because they feel like it. They have to point to a specific legal category, and if they're wrong or stretching it, they can be taken to court and lose — and they regularly do lose. The CIA, FBI, State Department, and others have all been forced by judges to release material they tried to withhold. That's meaningfully different from "the government decides what you get to know," even if it's less than "you get everything you ask for." **A lot of what actually is disclosed *because of* FOIA has been enormously consequential.** Torture memos, drone strike legal justifications, surveillance program details, environmental violations, workplace safety failures — all of this became public specifically because someone forced the issue through this imperfect system. The system has real flaws, but it's also produced real accountability, over and over, for decades. That's not nothing. **The depressing framing and the hopeful framing are both incomplete on their own.** "Full transparency" was never true, so believing it was would set you up to feel betrayed once you actually filed a request and hit a redaction. But "they hide whatever they want" isn't accurate either — there's a real legal fight built into the system, and it's winnable, which is why journalists and watchdog groups keep using it. I think the version worth holding onto isn't "the government must tell us everything" (which isn't true) or "they can hide anything" (also not true) — it's something more like: *the government has to justify its secrecy, in public, in front of a judge if necessary, and that justification can be challenged and often fails.* That's a more effortful, less clean form of accountability than the ideal you're describing, but it's also not nothing — and it's the actual tool people use to pry real information loose when it matters.
